TITLE:: NdefRack
summary:: a modular rack where every Ndef is a module and every cable is a JITLib mapping
categories:: Libraries>NdefRack
related:: Classes/NdefRackModule, Classes/NdefRackLib, Classes/NdefRackSpec, Classes/NdefRackGUI, Classes/Ndef

DESCRIPTION::
A node-based patch window for Ndefs, with no module definitions: every Ndef is a
module, every one of its controls is an input, and every cable is a mapping in
an Ndef's node map.

code::
s.boot;
NdefRack.gui;

Ndef(\osc, NdefRackLib(\verbos));     // a module from the library
Ndef(\lfo, NdefRackLib(\lfo8));       // another
Ndef(\osc).set(\levels, Ndef(\lfo));  // a cable - drawn in the rack at once
NdefRack.play(\osc);
::

Everything is on the class: there is one rack per session, watching the Ndefs
of one server - link::Classes/Server::.default unless link::#*server:: says
otherwise.

The patch is never stored here. It is read from each Ndef's node map whenever
something asks, so a cable made in code and a cable dragged in the window are
the same thing. The only state of the rack's own is where each module sits in
the window.

subsection:: Modules

The rack watches code::Ndef.dictFor(s)::. Ndef announces nothing when a new key
appears, so new Ndefs are found by looking, four times a second while
link::#*watch:: is on - the window turns it on. Everything else reaches a module
straight away: each link::Classes/NdefRackModule:: is a dependant of its Ndef,
and a set, a map, a new source or a play is news the moment it happens.

An Ndef is shown while it has a source or a bus. An Ndef that is cleared leaves
the rack, and comes back - to the same place in the window - when it is defined
again.

subsection:: Cables

link::#*connect:: is code::Ndef(\dst).set(\input, Ndef(\src)):: and nothing
more. It works across rates, and a wide source fills an array control from its
first value; into a single control only the first channel lands, and the
controls next to it are not touched.

link::#*disconnect:: is the one thing the rack has to get right. It is
code::unmap::, then the control's value set again - the value it had before the
cable came, or the default for a cable made in code. code::unset:: alone leaves
the server reading the bus: it sends back what the node map holds for the key,
which for a patched input is the source Ndef.

code::
NdefRack.connect(\lfo, \osc, \levels);
NdefRack.cables;
NdefRack.disconnect(\osc, \levels);
::

subsection:: Voices

A library module written for them - see link::Classes/NdefRackLib:: - can play
several voices at once: as many copies of itself in its one Ndef, a channel per
voice, every control a value per voice set by one bar. A cable with voices into
a module with as many goes voice to voice; a mono cable into a control with
voices goes in once per voice, code::Ndef(\lfo) ! 4::, so every voice hears it;
a cable with voices into a mono input brings the first alone.

Voices pass down the chain: a cable with voices into a control with a value per
voice makes the module as wide as the cable, and when the cable goes it is back
to the voices given it (link::#*voiceFloor::). It follows every cable made or
taken out - in code too - and every change of width of what feeds it.

code::
NdefRack.make(\wave, \pad, 4);       // four voices
NdefRack.setVoices(\pad, 8);        // eight, everything patched kept
NdefRack.voices(\pad);              // -> 8
::

link::#*setVoices:: keeps every cable into the module and out of it, with no gap:
the module moves to a bus of the new width and the rack sends again whatever
reads it, in the same moment - JITLib's own way of doing that loses readers.
The module's state starts over, and so does that of what it feeds.

subsection:: Buses

The library's code::bus:: module reads a bus: one of BusManager's by name - an
audio bus at audio rate, a control bus at control rate, as many channels as the
bus has - or a bus index of your own, at the width and rate you give it.
BusManager is looked up at run time, never needed; without it, an index still
works. In the window, the bus is picked in the drop-down on the module's face.

code::
NdefRack.make(\bus, \in, bus: 16, channels: 2);   // audio buses 16 and 17
NdefRack.bus(\in);                                // -> 16
NdefRack.setBus(\in, 20);                         // 20 and 21: the same width and rate
::

code::
BusManager.add(\drums, 2);
NdefRack.make(\bus, bus: \drums);     // Ndef(\drums), reading it
NdefRack.bus(\drums);                 // -> \drums
::

A BusManager bus is followed by its name: when BusManager moves the bus - a boot,
a layout, the name added again at another width - the module gets the source for
the bus as it is now, kept patched as link::#*setVoices:: keeps a module. A name
BusManager does not know is silence until it does; a bus taken away leaves the
module reading where it was. An index of your own stays where it is.

code::send::, code::throw::, code::sendpan::, code::replace::, code::blend:: and
code::insert:: write to a bus, picked, moved and saved the same way. What comes
in at their code::in:: goes to the bus - added by the first three, in place of
what is on it by the others - and their output passes the signal on, so a send
sits on the way. They write whether or not the module plays. With no bus, or a
name BusManager does not know, they write nowhere.

code::
BusManager.add(\verb, 2);
NdefRack.make(\wave, \osc);
NdefRack.make(\send, bus: \verb);       // Ndef(\send), writing to it
NdefRack.connect(\osc, \send, \in);     // a mono cable feeds both sides of the bus
NdefRack.bus(\send);                    // -> \verb
::

code::replace::, code::blend:: and code::insert:: act only on what modules made
before them have written to the bus in this block; what is made later, and every
module played onto the bus later, lands on top. The rack never reorders
modules: make these last.

subsection:: Copy, paste and delete

A copy is a new Ndef with the same source, slot by slot - the same function
object, so a copy of a library module is still one - and the values its controls
show, under a free name: code::osc:: pastes as code::osc_2::. The cables
between the copied modules are made again between the copies; a cable from or
to anything outside the copy stays behind.

code::
NdefRack.copy([\lfo, \osc]);
NdefRack.paste(Point(400, 300));      // -> [\lfo_2, \osc_2], the group's top left there
NdefRack.duplicate([\osc]);           // copy and paste in one, the clipboard untouched
::

Removing writes everything down first - sources, values, places, every cable
touching the modules, whether they were playing - so link::#*undoRemove:: puts
the last removal back as it was. Made again, each module is last in the order
the groups run in: on a bus with a code::replace::, code::blend:: or
code::insert::, a writer brought back lands on top of it. A writer deleted fades
out of its bus in its fadeTime.

code::
NdefRack.removeAll([\lfo_2, \osc_2]);
NdefRack.undoRemove;
::

subsection:: Notifications

What the window listens to, and what anything else showing the rack can listen
to - through code::SimpleController(NdefRack)::. They can arrive from any
thread: code::NdefRack.set:: in a routine sends them from that routine.

table::
## strong::message:: || strong::argument:: || strong::when::
## code::\modules:: || || a module arrived or left
## code::\module:: || key || a module's controls, rate or channels changed - its source was redefined
## code::\state:: || key || a value or a cable of that module changed
## code::\monitor:: || key || it started or stopped playing
## code::\position:: || key || it was moved with link::#*moveTo::
## code::\positions:: || || a patch moved them all
## code::\patches:: || || a patch was saved or loaded
## code::\watching:: || || link::#*watch:: or link::#*unwatch::
::


CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: gui
Open the window, or bring it to the front. link::Classes/NdefRackGUI::.
ARGUMENT:: bounds
a link::Classes/Rect:: for a new window.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackGUI::.

METHOD:: guiObj
The open window, or nil.

METHOD:: server
The server whose Ndefs the rack shows. link::Classes/Server::.default unless set.
Setting it forgets every module and reads the new server's Ndefs.

METHOD:: proxySpace
The link::Classes/ProxySpace:: Ndef keeps for the server, or nil before the first
Ndef there. Looking does not make one.

METHOD:: watch
Start watching: a link::Classes/SkipJack:: looks for new and cleared Ndefs every
link::#*pollTime:: seconds. It survives cmd + period. The window starts it.

METHOD:: unwatch
Stop watching. Modules already in the rack go on hearing from their Ndefs.

METHOD:: watching
Whether the rack is watching.

METHOD:: pollTime
How often to look for new Ndefs, in seconds. 0.25.

METHOD:: scan
Look now: compare the Ndefs there are with the modules there are. The questions
about the whole rack - link::#*names::, link::#*cables::, the code export - do
this first, and so does link::#*at:: for a name it does not know yet, so an Ndef
defined in the same block as the line that patches it is found without waiting
for the poll.

METHOD:: modules
Every module, key -> link::Classes/NdefRackModule::.

METHOD:: at
The module for an Ndef.
ARGUMENT:: name
a Symbol, or the Ndef itself.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, or nil.

METHOD:: includes
Whether there is a module of that name.

METHOD:: names
Every module's key, in the order they arrived.

METHOD:: size
How many modules there are.

METHOD:: cables
Every cable, as Events: code::(from: \lfo, to: \osc, param: \levels, kind: \map)::
for each Ndef mapped into another one's control - with code::spread: true:: for a
mono cable spread over voices - and
code::(from: \lfo, to: \osc, kind: \read):: for each Ndef a source function reads
by itself, with code::Ndef.ar(\lfo):: - a wire the window shows dashed and cannot
unplug. A cable from an Ndef outside the rack is not listed.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: of link::Classes/Event::s.

METHOD:: connect
Plug one module's output into another's input:
code::Ndef(to).set(param, Ndef(from))::. A mono source into a control with a
value per voice goes in once per voice. An input the library marks as code::follow::
- code::splay::'s and code::sum::'s code::in:: - makes its module take as many
voices as the source has channels.
ARGUMENT:: from
the source, a Symbol or the Ndef.
ARGUMENT:: to
the destination.
ARGUMENT:: param
the name of the control.
RETURNS:: the destination's link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, or nil if anything was not there.

METHOD:: disconnect
Unplug an input: code::unmap::, then the value back - the one it had before the
cable came, or the function's default. The default goes back with code::unset::
once the unmap has taken the key out, so the node map is left as if nothing had
been patched.
ARGUMENT:: to
the module.
ARGUMENT:: param
the input.

METHOD:: unplug
Every cable into the module and out of it.

METHOD:: set
code::Ndef(name).set(param, value):: - what a bar in the window does.

METHOD:: play
Play an audio rate module on the stereo pair: a mono one on both sides, a stereo
one left and right, anything wider - voices - spread across the pair the way
link::Classes/Splay:: spreads them, levelled by 1/sqrt of the count. After that
it plays wherever JITLib's monitor last sent it, as long as that still covers
every channel. Refused for a control rate module.

METHOD:: stop
Stop playing it.

METHOD:: togglePlay
Play it if it is not playing; stop it if it is. The window's play button.

METHOD:: make
A library module as an Ndef.
code::
NdefRack.make(\verbos);          // Ndef(\verbos), or \verbos_2 if that is taken
NdefRack.make(\verbos, \osc);    // Ndef(\osc)
NdefRack.make(\wave, \pad, 4);    // four voices
NdefRack.make(\bus, \in, bus: 16, channels: 2);   // audio buses 16 and 17
::
ARGUMENT:: kind
the library key, see link::Classes/NdefRackLib::.
ARGUMENT:: name
the Ndef's key; if nil, the next free one after code::kind:: - or, for a bus
module reading a BusManager bus, after the bus's name. One writing to a bus is
called after its kind.
ARGUMENT:: voices
how many voices, for a module written for them; 1 if not given.
ARGUMENT:: bus
for a bus module - code::bus::, code::send:: and the others: the name of the
BusManager bus it reads or writes to, a bus index, or a link::Classes/Bus::; nil
for none.
ARGUMENT:: channels
for a bus index: how many channels; 1 if not given.
ARGUMENT:: rate
for a bus index: code::\audio:: (if not given) or code::\control::.
ARGUMENT:: speakers
for a module panning among speakers - code::vbap::, code::ambi::: the name of a
setup in link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::' table; code::\stereo:: if not given.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/Ndef::.

METHOD:: ndef
The Ndef of that name on the rack's server, with a new source if one is given.
The source decides the width: an Ndef that was wider or narrower takes the new
one's, and whatever reads it stays patched.

METHOD:: voices
How many voices a module plays: 1 for a mono module, and for an Ndef of your own.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Integer::, or nil when there is no such module.

METHOD:: canVoice
Whether the module is a library module written for voices.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.

METHOD:: voiceFloor
The voices given a module - by link::#*make::, link::#*setVoices::, a patch file
or a width given in code: the fewest it plays. A cable with more voices into one
of its controls with a value per voice makes it wider while it is there.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Integer::, 1 when none were given.

METHOD:: setVoices
Play at least that many voices - more while a cable with more voices is patched
into a control with a value per voice; the modules it feeds follow its new width. Each control's value goes to every voice, a row of sliders
is cut or lengthened with its last value, a mono cable into the module is spread
over the new voices, and every cable out of it stays patched, with no gap; a
module that is playing goes on playing, spread over its new channels. The module
is given a new source, so its own state starts over, and so does that of the
modules it feeds. Warns, and does nothing, for a module without voices.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: n
the number of voices, 1 to link::Classes/NdefRackLib#*maxVoices::.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, or nil.

METHOD:: setValues
Set a control from text: a number, an array, any expression giving one; a
link::Classes/Function:: of the voice and the number of voices,
code::{ |i, n| 110 * (i + 1) }::, once per voice; or a generator by name with
its numbers - code::lin 200 800::, code::exp 100 3200::, code::spread 0 0.1::,
code::rand 200 800::, code::harm 110::, code::alt 0 1::, code::chord 0 4 7:: (see
link::#*generators::). A function or a generator is remembered and worked out
again whenever the module's voices change, as long as the control still holds
what it gave and is not patched.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control.
ARGUMENT:: text
a link::Classes/String::.
RETURNS:: the value set, or nil when the text gives no number.

METHOD:: showVoices
Show a control with a value per voice as a slider per voice in the window, in
place of its one bar - while the module plays more than one voice; the choice
stays through changes of voices.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control.
ARGUMENT:: flag
false for one bar again.

METHOD:: showsVoices
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control.
RETURNS:: whether link::#*showVoices:: shows it as a slider per voice.

METHOD:: valuesText
The function or generator a control's value came from, while the control still
holds what it gave.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: param
the control.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String::, or nil.

METHOD:: generators
The generators link::#*setValues:: knows, name -> code::{ |n, args| ... }::
answering a value for each of n voices from the numbers typed after the name.
Add your own with code::put::.

METHOD:: bus
What a bus module reads, or writes to - what link::#*setBus:: takes.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
RETURNS:: the BusManager name, a link::Classes/Symbol::; the bus index, an link::Classes/Integer::; or nil for any other module, and for a bus module on none.

METHOD:: canBus
Whether the module is a bus module.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.

METHOD:: setBus
Read another bus, or write to another: one of BusManager's, or a bus index. A bus
of another width or rate is a new source, made so that every cable out of the
module stays patched, and the cables into it are kept; a playing module goes on
playing. A module writing to a bus fades out of the old one. Warns, and does
nothing, for a module that is not a bus module.
code::
NdefRack.make(\bus, \in, bus: 16, channels: 2);
NdefRack.setBus(\in, 20);             // buses 20, 21 - the width and rate kept
NdefRack.setBus(\in, 20, 1);          // only 20
NdefRack.setBus(\in, 8, 4, \control); // control buses 8 to 11
::
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: bus
the BusManager bus's name, a bus index, or a link::Classes/Bus::; nil for none.
ARGUMENT:: channels
for a bus index: how many channels; the ones it has now if not given.
ARGUMENT:: rate
for a bus index: code::\audio:: or code::\control::; the one it has now if not given.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, or nil.

METHOD:: speakers
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
RETURNS:: the name of the speaker setup a module pans among, or nil.

METHOD:: canSpeakers
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
RETURNS:: whether it is a library module panning among speakers - code::vbap::, code::ambi::.

METHOD:: setSpeakers
Pan among other speakers. A setup with another number of speakers is a new
source at a new width, made so that every cable out of the module stays patched;
the cables into it and its values are kept, and a playing module goes on
playing - speaker by speaker on the outputs when the server has as many, folded
onto the stereo pair when it has not. Warns, and does nothing, for a module that
does not pan among speakers.
code::
NdefRack.make(\vbap, \pan, speakers: \quad);   // 4 channels
NdefRack.setSpeakers(\pan, \octo);              // 8
NdefRack.setSpeakers(\pan, '5.1');              // 6, the fourth the sub's
::
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: setup
a name from link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers#*names::, an link::Classes/NdefRackSpeakers::, or nil for code::\stereo::.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, or nil.

METHOD:: recorder
BusRecorder, when it is installed - looked up by name, never needed.
RETURNS:: the class, or nil.

METHOD:: canRecord
Whether a module can be recorded: an audio module, with BusRecorder installed.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.

METHOD:: setRecorded
Make an audio module one of BusRecorder's sources - its Ndef's bus, under the
module's name, or a free name beside a source of yours that has it - or, with
code::flag:: false, take it away. A module already recorded is armed again.
When the module changes width the source moves to its new bus, in a take in
progress too; deleting the module takes the source away, and
link::#*undoRemove:: brings it back.
code::
NdefRack.setRecorded(\pad);
BusRecorder.record;
::
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.
ARGUMENT:: flag
true to record it, false to stop.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackModule::, or nil.

METHOD:: isRecorded
Whether BusRecorder has a source reading the module's bus.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.

METHOD:: recordSource
BusRecorder's source reading the module's bus, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: name
the module.

METHOD:: remove
Take a module out: unplug every cable into it and out of it, clear the Ndef, and
remove it from the ProxySpace - as if it had never been defined. Code that
defines it again brings it back, and so does link::#*undoRemove::.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackModule:: that was removed.

METHOD:: removeAll
Several at once - the window's delete. Everything about them is written down
before anything goes.
ARGUMENT:: names
Symbols or Ndefs.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/NdefRackModule::s removed.

METHOD:: undoRemove
The last removal back: the same names where they are free, the same sources,
values, places, colours and pages, every cable - those to modules that are still in the rack
too - and playing if they were. Once.
RETURNS:: the keys brought back.

METHOD:: canUndoRemove
Whether there is a removal to undo.

METHOD:: copy
Put what it takes to make these modules again on the clipboard, with the cables
between them.
ARGUMENT:: names
Symbols or Ndefs.
RETURNS:: the clipboard, an link::Classes/Event::.

METHOD:: cut
link::#*copy::, then link::#*removeAll::.

METHOD:: paste
Make the clipboard's modules again as new Ndefs under free names, with the
cables between them. Nothing pasted plays until you play it.
ARGUMENT:: at
where the top left of the group lands, a link::Classes/Point::; 40 pixels down
and right of the originals when nil.
RETURNS:: the new keys, in the clipboard's order.

METHOD:: duplicate
Copy and paste in one, leaving the clipboard as it was.
ARGUMENT:: names
the modules.
ARGUMENT:: offset
how far from the originals, a link::Classes/Point::. 40 @ 40.
RETURNS:: the new keys.

METHOD:: clipboard
What link::#*copy:: took, or nil.

METHOD:: hide
METHOD:: unhide
Leave an Ndef out of the rack - a master bus, a utility nobody patches - and let
it back in.

METHOD:: hidden
The keys left out.

METHOD:: positionOf
Where the window draws a module, a link::Classes/Point::. Kept after the module
goes, so an Ndef cleared and defined again comes back to the same place.

METHOD:: moveTo
Move a module in the window.

METHOD:: positions
Every position, key -> Point.

METHOD:: bendOf
Where the window bends a cable drawn as a flow chart: code::[x]::, the x of its
one vertical run, or code::[x1, y, x2]::, its two vertical runs and the lane
between them - see link::Classes/NdefRackRouter::. nil when the window finds the
way itself. Like a position it belongs to the window; it is kept here so a patch
file keeps it.
ARGUMENT:: from
The module the cable comes from.
ARGUMENT:: to
The module it goes into.
ARGUMENT:: param
The input.
RETURNS:: an link::Classes/Array:: or nil

METHOD:: bend
Set where a cable bends; nil gives the way back to the window. Notifies
code::\bends::. The window does this when a cable is dragged.
ARGUMENT:: from
The module the cable comes from.
ARGUMENT:: to
The module it goes into.
ARGUMENT:: param
The input.
ARGUMENT:: bend
code::[x]::, code::[x1, y, x2]:: or nil.
code::
NdefRack.bend(\osc, \flt, \in, [300]);   // its vertical run at x 300
NdefRack.bend(\osc, \flt, \in, nil);     // the window's way again
::

METHOD:: addComment
A box of text in the window. Like a position it belongs to the window and to no
Ndef; it is kept here so a patch file keeps it. Notifies code::\comments::.
ARGUMENT:: text
What it says.
ARGUMENT:: point
Its top left, in the rack's coordinates.
ARGUMENT:: size
Its width and height, a link::Classes/Point::. 220 @ 80.
RETURNS:: its id, a link::Classes/Symbol:: - code::\comment1::, code::\comment2::...
code::
NdefRack.addComment("the loop: seed, fold, split, tilt, back", Point(40, 20), Point(300, 50));
::

METHOD:: commentIds
The ids of the comments, in the order they were made.

METHOD:: commentText
What a comment says.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: commentRect
Where a comment is, a link::Classes/Rect:: in the rack's coordinates.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: setComment
Write in a comment.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.
ARGUMENT:: text
What it says now.

METHOD:: placeComment
Move or resize a comment.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.
ARGUMENT:: rect
A link::Classes/Rect:: in the rack's coordinates.

METHOD:: removeComment
Delete a comment.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: clearComments
Delete every comment.

METHOD:: comments
Every comment, id -> code::(text:, rect:)::.

METHOD:: setCompact
Show a module compact in the window - its main row and only the controls away
from their defaults, set or patched - or not. Notifies code::\compact::.
ARGUMENT:: name
The module.
ARGUMENT:: flag
true for compact.

METHOD:: isCompact
Whether the window shows a module compact.
ARGUMENT:: name
The module.

METHOD:: compacted
The keys shown compact. A removed module's goes with it.

METHOD:: setPage
Show a page of a module's controls - counted from 0 - when it has pages: more
controls past its main inputs than link::Classes/NdefRackModuleView#*pageRows::.
Notifies code::\page::.
ARGUMENT:: name
The module.
ARGUMENT:: page
The page, from 0.

METHOD:: pageOf
The page of a module the window shows; 0 when none was set.
ARGUMENT:: name
The module.

METHOD:: pageNums
The pages set, key -> page. A removed module's goes with it.

METHOD:: setColor
Mark a module with a colour: a name of link::Classes/NdefRackGUI#*palette::, nil
for none. The window draws its edge in it and tints its title; what it puts out
keeps its colour. Notifies code::\colors::.
ARGUMENT:: name
The module.
ARGUMENT:: color
A colour name, or nil.

METHOD:: colorOf
The colour a module is marked with, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: name
The module.

METHOD:: setCableColor
Mark a cable with a colour: the window draws stripes of it over the colour of
what it carries. nil for none.
ARGUMENT:: from
The module it comes from.
ARGUMENT:: to
The module it goes into.
ARGUMENT:: param
The input.
ARGUMENT:: color
A colour name, or nil.

METHOD:: cableColorOf
The colour a cable is marked with, or nil.
ARGUMENT:: from
The module it comes from.
ARGUMENT:: to
The module it goes into.
ARGUMENT:: param
The input.

METHOD:: moduleColors
Every module's colour, key -> name. A removed module's goes with it.

METHOD:: cableColors
Every cable's colour, code::[from, to, param] -> name::.

METHOD:: addBox
A network box: a coloured box behind the modules in the window, with a name.
Dragging its title moves it and what is in it. It belongs to no Ndef; a patch
file keeps it. Notifies code::\boxes::.
ARGUMENT:: name
Its name.
ARGUMENT:: rect
Where it is, in the rack's coordinates.
ARGUMENT:: color
A colour name of link::Classes/NdefRackGUI#*palette::. code::\teal::.
RETURNS:: its id - code::\box1::, code::\box2::...
code::
NdefRack.addBox("the loop", Rect(40, 40, 600, 300), \purple);
::

METHOD:: boxIds
The ids of the boxes, in the order they were made - the order they are drawn in,
a later one over an older one.

METHOD:: boxRect
Where a box is.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: placeBox
Move or resize a box - only the box; the window moves what is in it.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.
ARGUMENT:: rect
Where it goes.

METHOD:: renameBox
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.
ARGUMENT:: name
Its name now.

METHOD:: colorBox
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.
ARGUMENT:: color
A colour name.

METHOD:: removeBox
Delete a box; what was in it stays.
ARGUMENT:: id
Its id.

METHOD:: clearBoxes
Delete every box.

METHOD:: boxes
Every box, id -> code::(name:, rect:, color:)::.

METHOD:: clearBends
Every cable's way given back to the window; the window's strong::arrange:: does
it.

METHOD:: bends
Every bend, code::[from, to, param] -> bend::. A removed module's bends go with
it.

METHOD:: patchCode
The rack as plain JITLib code, which runs where NdefRack is not installed:
every module's source as text - a library module's own, at the width it has,
code::{ |n| { ... } }.value(16):: for sixteen voices - each Ndef set to take
that width (code::reshaping = \elastic::), one code::Ndef(...).set(...)::
line per module with the numbers and cables in its node map, and
code::Ndef(...).play:: for what is playing. Your own Ndefs are written from their
function's text when the interpreter kept it, named otherwise. code::vbap:: and
code::ambi:: still need the rack for their speaker setup; controller bindings
run only where link::Classes/NdefRackKtl:: is there.
ARGUMENT:: names
only these modules; all of them if nil.
ARGUMENT:: sources
false leaves out the lines that make the modules.
RETURNS:: a link::Classes/String:: that compiles.

METHOD:: postPatch
link::#*patchCode::, in the post window.

METHOD:: patchDocument
link::#*patchCode:: in a new, unsaved document in the IDE - the window's
strong::patch as code::. Without an IDE it is posted instead.
RETURNS:: the link::Classes/Document::, or the code when it was posted.

METHOD:: codeDocument
Any code in a new document of its own; posted without an IDE.
ARGUMENT:: code
a String.
ARGUMENT:: title
the document's title.

METHOD:: savePatch
Write the rack to a file: modules (library ones by key, interpreter-written ones
with the text of their function), settings, cables, positions, what plays. A
bare name goes in link::#*patchDir:: as code::name.scd:: - never over an
example; a path with a slash is used as given.
RETURNS:: the path written.

METHOD:: loadPatch
Read a patch back. Library modules are made where missing; an Ndef of your own
comes back from its saved text only when nothing defines it now. Then the values,
the cables - an input the patch leaves empty is unplugged - the positions, and
what plays.
ARGUMENT:: name
the name or path given to link::#*savePatch::.
ARGUMENT:: clear
true also removes the modules the patch does not have.

METHOD:: asDict
The patch as an link::Classes/Event::, the way link::#*savePatch:: writes it.

METHOD:: patches
The patch files on disk - yours newest first, then the examples by name: what
the window's menu offers.

METHOD:: examplePatches
The example patches that come with the rack, by name.

METHOD:: patchDir
code::patches-local/:: next to the class files, where your patches are saved.
Git ignores it.

METHOD:: examplesDir
code::patches/:: next to the class files: the example patches.

METHOD:: ensurePatchDir
link::#*patchDir::, made if it is not there.

METHOD:: patchPath
Where a patch of that name is read: a path with a slash as given, a bare name
in link::#*patchDir:: first, else the example of that name.


EXAMPLES::

code::
s.boot;
NdefRack.gui;

// a module of your own: four controls, a stereo output
(
Ndef(\pad, {
	var sig = Saw.ar(\freq.kr(110, spec: [40, 400, \exp]) * [1, 1.01]);
	RLPF.ar(sig, \cutoff.kr(1200), \rq.kr(0.3)) * \amp.kr(0.1)
});
);

// a slow sweep for its cutoff: lo and hi are the range it swings over
Ndef(\sweep, NdefRackLib(\lfo)).set(\rate, 0.1, \lo, 300, \hi, 3000);
NdefRack.connect(\sweep, \pad, \cutoff);
NdefRack.play(\pad);

// the same patch, as code
NdefRack.postPatch;

// unplugged: the cutoff is back at 1200
NdefRack.disconnect(\pad, \cutoff);

// saved and brought back
NdefRack.savePatch("help example");
NdefRack.loadPatch("help example");

NdefRack.stop(\pad);
::
